Dear all,

I am trying to use Procrustes ANOVA to examine measurement error. I have three haplogroups and from each group I chose 2 individuals, photographed and digitized each of them twice (totally 24 images without object symmetry). I used the following codes to assess both photographing and digitizing error:

data <- readland.tps("aligned.tps", specID = "ID") ##read the aligned.tps file

gpa <- gpagen(data)  ## another GPA in geomorph to have  partial Procrustes superimposition

individuals <- dimnames(gpa$coords)[[3]]  ## extracting list of individuals(Beh_1_1;Beh_1_2;Beh_2_1;Beh_2_2; etc)

## trying to get classifier vectors from the list of individuals:

library(reshape2)

b <-colsplit(string=individuals, pattern="_", names=c("Ind", "photo", "rep"))

Ind <- b$Ind

photo <- b$photo

rep <- b$rep

Ind.er <- procD.lm(gpa$coords ~ factor(Ind))

Ind.er

photo.er <http://photo.er> <- procD.lm(gpa$coords ~ Ind:photo)

photo.er <http://photo.er>

rep.er <http://rep.er> <- procD.lm(gpa$coords ~ Ind:photo:rep)

rep.er <http://rep.er>

I would be grateful if anyone could tell me whether the codes are correct and giving me some advice about these questions:

1- Which one gives me information about both photographing and digitizing error, Ind.er, photo.er <http://photo.er> or rep.er <http://rep.er>?

2- More importantly, how should I report the results to say if there is a measurement error or not?

3- Generally, to assess measurement error, is this a good idea to include all the variations in my haplogroups by choosing two individuals from each of them or is this better to limit the shape variations for this analysis?

I greatly appreciate your help,

Ellie

